WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:03.860
Luxembourg is a small country.
It's about 50 miles by 35 miles,

00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:06.540
with just over 600,000 people
living in it.

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:08.380
And here in the capital,
Luxembourg City,

00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:11.820
around 200,000 people
commute into work every day,

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:13.460
half from outside the country,

00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:16.800
but less than 20% of them
travel by public transit.

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:22.480
But from Sunday, March 1st,
all public transit in the entire country will be free.

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:25.720
Pretty soon, there'll be no ticket machines,
unless you want an international ticket.

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:28.140
There'll be no fare dodgers,
unless you count people

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:32.040
who are sneaking into the first-class train cabins
that you will still have to pay for.

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:37.320
And on the face of it, free public transit
seems like an easy decision.

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:40.840
Get people out of cars
and into buses and trains.

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:43.440
But it's a little more
complicated than that.

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:46.320
- We are the country,
besides Qatar,

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:51.160
with the highest degree of
cars per household, in Luxembourg,

00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:55.760
and we have definitely a big problem,
especially in peak hours,

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:59.460
with enormous congestion problems.

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:04.280
We have a problem also that
quality-of-life in our cities, in our villages,

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:06.000
it's really worsened.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:09.200
That comes because
Luxembourg is a country where

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:15.260
you had not only the highest average
of economic growth in the last 25 years,

00:01:15.260 --> 00:01:20.240
but also our population growth
is the highest in the European Union.

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:23.960
Having mobility behaviour
that is mainly based on individual cars

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:25.560
cannot really function anymore,

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:29.460
and at the end, it's also a problem then
for our economy itself.

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:31.900
- Making public transit free
is not a new idea.

00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:34.340
There are quite a few cities around the world
who've already done it,

00:01:34.340 --> 00:01:37.880
and a lot of Estonia now has
free public transit for residents.

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:41.840
Luxembourg, though, is the first country
to abolish fares entirely,

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:46.100
and partly that's because it can afford to do it.
The country is a small, rich tax haven.

00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:49.380
The thing is, public transit here is
almost free already.

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:53.980
A ticket valid for a full day on every
bus, tram, and train in the country is €4.

00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:58.020
The whole transit network costs the government
€700 million a year to operate,

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:01.560
but all the tickets sold
add up to just 10% of that.

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:05.180
By comparison, London's transit network is
about half-funded by fares.

00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:07.180
So from the Luxembourg
government's perspective,

00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:11.940
there's not actually that much difference
between a €4 day ticket and free transit,

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:16.280
but "free transit" is the sort of headline
that gets you a lot of good publicity.

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:20.660
But hey, if transit is free,
why would you want to get around by car?

00:02:20.660 --> 00:02:23.380
Well, it's because
the transit here isn't great.

00:02:23.380 --> 00:02:25.560
It's okay,
by European standards.

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:28.680
The buses and trains will get you there.
I've had no problems as a tourist,

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:32.400
but the commuter lines are
already overcrowded in rush hour.

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:35.520
A lot of the rolling stock is dated,
and there is a litany of complaints

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:38.700
about the paths they take
and how they deal with breakdowns.

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:43.260
Driving here, even with the congestion,
is usually quicker and more convenient,

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:46.640
sometimes even for journeys
directly between city centres.

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:49.020
- So every year, there's more and more
people moving here,

00:02:49.020 --> 00:02:52.860
and the infrastructure system
is under a lot of strain.

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:57.060
So the needs are to make it work,
not to make it free.

00:02:57.060 --> 00:03:00.780
There's lots of research on this,
and what comes out over and over again

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:03.760
is that the fare is not
the main motivating factor.

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:07.460
The main motivating factor is
comfort, reliability, and safety.

00:03:07.460 --> 00:03:10.220
No one ever knows if the trains are
actually going to be on time.

00:03:10.220 --> 00:03:12.780
It has happened that people are standing
for an hour and a half

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:17.320
on the platform waiting for the train,
with no substitute buses or anything.

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:21.700
So that's an extreme case, obviously,
but if one wants to be somewhere on time,

00:03:21.700 --> 00:03:24.880
one has to take this into account,
that these things do happen.

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:28.320
- The cities who are doing this
investment into public transport,

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:29.940
into free public transport,

00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:33.960
that's really the bit of cities that,
from a competitive point of view,

00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:36.160
will be, in the world,
the winning cities.

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:40.320
Even if I say it's free to use,
somebody pays it.

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:46.300
So at the end, the 700 million euros
will be paid by the taxpayers in general.

00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:50.980
Someone, for example, with minimum income
pays no taxes.

00:03:50.980 --> 00:03:54.640
So he has the public transport,
really, for free,

00:03:54.640 --> 00:04:00.240
but somebody who pays high taxes,
he pays much more.

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:02.366
- That's an important point:
for someone like me,

00:04:02.366 --> 00:04:06.640
who's a tourist with a travel budget,
€4 for a day pass isn't a big issue,

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:10.060
but for someone on minimum wage
who has to commute an hour, or two hours

00:04:10.060 --> 00:04:14.780
from wherever housing's affordable,
that makes a big difference.

00:04:14.780 --> 00:04:18.160
- I don't think the key social issue
in Luxembourg is the price of the transit.

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:20.240
One needs to look at the housing costs.

00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:24.940
The increasing prices of housing
are pushing people to the border regions.

00:04:24.940 --> 00:04:29.980
So it might be financially more sound
to live in Belgium, or France and Germany,

00:04:29.980 --> 00:04:33.680
buy a house, or rent a house,
and buy a couple of cars,

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:37.260
than it would be to live here in Luxembourg
and use the transit.

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:41.280
It needs investment,
and it needs investment now. It's behind.

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:44.540
And so Luxembourg needs to catch up
and plan for the future.

00:04:44.540 --> 00:04:48.260
- You must see the
free public transport additionally

00:04:48.260 --> 00:04:50.540
to the investments
that we are doing

00:04:50.540 --> 00:04:54.780
in the improvement of the network
and the quality of the public transport.

00:04:54.780 --> 00:05:01.200
If you only introduce free public transport,
that will change not very much behaviour.

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:05.980
It can only function if you do it combined
with a complete strategy

00:05:05.980 --> 00:05:10.360
that will switch from individual mobility
to multi-modality,

00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:13.840
so even including pedestrians, cyclists,

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:19.680
and if you invest at the same time, a lot of money,
in the infrastructure of public transport,

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:21.860
so that at the end,
you can combine everything.

00:05:21.860 --> 00:05:29.260
We say, if we built an infrastructure,
we will build it to move people and not to move cars.

00:05:29.260 --> 00:05:31.480
- I'm not saying free transit
is a bad thing.

00:05:31.480 --> 00:05:33.540
I think it's a great idea, personally,

00:05:33.540 --> 00:05:36.900
but having good, frequent,
well-connected transit

00:05:36.900 --> 00:05:40.280
that isn't too crowded to board
is also important.

00:05:40.280 --> 00:05:43.020
The question isn't as binary as
"Should public transit be free?"

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:44.880
because in a utopia,
of course it would be.

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:46.960
It would also be autonomous,
zero-emission,

00:05:46.960 --> 00:05:50.440
and it would run from anywhere
to everywhere all the time.

00:05:50.440 --> 00:05:52.980
But until we're in
a post-scarcity society,

00:05:52.980 --> 00:05:56.140
the public are paying one way or another,
either through taxes or fares.

00:05:56.140 --> 00:06:00.240
The more important question is about
planning and long-term investment,

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:04.500
because if your bus is always late,
and your train's always cancelled,

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:07.220
it doesn't matter if they're free.

